{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CfcP1KDHEP3iQzoQqf3X8j","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Here are two excellent examples of manual flag parsing without cobra or the flag package:\n\n## Example 1: `cmd/head/main.go`\n\nThe `head` command demonstrates a clean pattern for parsing flags with values:\n\n**Short flags with values** (lines 204-251):\n```go\nfunc parseShortFlags(flags string, remaining []string, opts *options) (int, error) {\n\tconsumed := 0\n\tfor j := 0; j < len(flags); j++ {\n\t\tswitch flags[j] {\n\t\tcase 'n':\n\t\t\tvar val string\n\t\t\tif rest := flags[j+1:]; rest != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tval = rest  // -n10 form\n\t\t\t} else if len(remaining) > consumed {\n\t\t\t\tval = remaining[consumed]  // -n 10 form\n\t\t\t\tconsumed++\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option requires an argument -- 'n'\")\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcount, err := parseLineCount(val)\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn 0, err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\topts.count = count\n\t\t\topts.mode = modeLines\n\t\t\treturn consumed, nil\n```\n\n**Long flags with values** (lines 155-173):\n```go\ncase strings.HasPrefix(flag, \"--lines=\"):\n\tcount, err := parseLineCount(flag[len(\"--lines=\"):])\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\topts.count = count\n\topts.mode = modeLines\n\treturn 1, nil\ncase flag == \"--lines\":\n\tif len(remaining) < 2 {\n\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option '--lines' requires an argument\")\n\t}\n\tcount, err := parseLineCount(remaining[1])\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\topts.count = count\n\topts.mode = modeLines\n\treturn 2, nil\n```\n\nThe key pattern: consume flag(s) and return how many args were used (including the flag itself).\n\n## Example 2: `cmd/fold/main.go`\n\nThe `fold` command shows a simpler approach for extracting flag values (lines 115-135):\n\n```go\nfunc setWidth(val string, remaining []string, opts *options) (int, error) {\n\tif val == \"\" {\n\t\tif len(remaining) > 0 {\n\t\t\tval = remaining[0]\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option requires an argument -- 'w'\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tn, err := strconv.Atoi(val)\n\t\tif err != nil || n <= 0 {\n\t\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"invalid number of columns: '%s'\", val)\n\t\t}\n\t\topts.width = n\n\t\treturn 1, nil  // consumed one additional argument\n\t}\n\tn, err := strconv.Atoi(val)\n\tif err != nil || n <= 0 {\n\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"invalid number of columns: '%s'\", val)\n\t}\n\topts.width = n\n\treturn 0, nil  // no additional arguments consumed\n}\n```\n\n**Key pattern:** Check if the value is already attached to the flag (`-w80`), and if not, take it from the next argument. Return how many additional args were consumed (0 or 1).\n\nBoth examples use manual os.Args iteration with a counter that tracks position, parse the flag value (either attached like `-n10` or separate like `-n 10`), and return the count of consumed arguments for proper iteration advancement."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90e916e3-9a57-43df-b341-b74db7c6ff8c","uuid":"b43d855e-bdc9-4917-96f0-0027f90377ee","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:07:39.206Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5268, look at how other cmd/ utilities parse flags manually (without cobra or flag package). Search for patterns like `os.Args` parsing in cmd/ main.go files, especially ones that handle flags with values like `-t` or `-n`. I want to see 1-2 examples of how they extract flag values from os.Args. Quick search is fine.","agentId":"a3fbd43614fbbe580","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Here are two excellent examples of manual flag parsing without cobra or the flag package:\n\n## Example 1: `cmd/head/main.go`\n\nThe `head` command demonstrates a clean pattern for parsing flags with values:\n\n**Short flags with values** (lines 204-251):\n```go\nfunc parseShortFlags(flags